1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
This is the Git collaboration part of Task 6.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Two developers, or here two feature branches, were started from the same commit on develop.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
One added the hotel contact footer, the other added a footer showing the running release.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Look at this graph.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The two side lines branch from the same point and merge back one after the other.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
The common starting point is printed below.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
When this happened in the project, I saved the unedited terminal output.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Merging the second feature printed conflict in three files: style dot css, base dot html and the integration tests.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Git merged what it could, and stopped, saying the automatic merge failed.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The status lines starting with U U mean unmerged on both sides.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:07,000
To show that it is real, I reproduce it now in a disposable clone in the temporary folder, so the project is not touched.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:16,000
I clone the repository, go to the commit where the contact footer was already merged, and try to merge the release footer branch.

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Git reports the same three conflicts, exactly as in the saved evidence.

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
This is how Git shows a conflict inside the file.

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The line with seven less than signs and HEAD starts our side, the contact footer.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The line of equals signs separates the two versions.

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The line with seven greater than signs ends the other side, the release footer.

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Git cannot know which to keep, so a person must decide.

19
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Here both lines are wanted.

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
This is the real resolved file from the actual merge commit.

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Both features were wanted, so the resolution keeps both lines: the contact line first, the release line below.

22
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The style sheet got one combined footer rule, and each line sits on its own row.

23
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Nothing from either developer was lost.

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
The third conflicted file was the test file.

25
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Both new tests were kept, one for the contact details and one for the release footer.

26
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:39,000
And the merge commit message documents the whole decision: what was in conflict in each file, what was kept, and that the linter was clean and all eighty nine tests passed before committing.

27
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,000
That is good practice.

28
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Back in the practice clone I abort the merge, which returns it to the state before the merge, then delete the clone.

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Then I show that my real project repository is unchanged.

30
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Now the release tags.

31
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
A tag is a permanent label on one commit that marks a release.

32
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
We have v1.0.0, the first complete version, and v1.1.0, which adds the nights column.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
For each tag I print the commit it points to, and the VERSION file as it was inside that tag.

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
So the tag really contains the released version.

35
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
What is the difference between a branch and a tag?

36
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
A branch is a moving line of development, it advances with every new commit.

37
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
A tag stays fixed on one commit.

38
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Proof: main has moved on eleven commits since the v1.1.0 tag, mostly documentation, but the tag still points to the same release commit.

39
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Git describe prints exactly that: the tag name, eleven commits ahead.


